<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Two decisions live here, and only one of them is about trust.

  TRUST: the system store and nothing else, which is Android's default and is restated so the
  debug block below has a file to live in. `debug-overrides` applies ONLY when android:debuggable is
  true, which a release build never is; it lets a debug build trust a CA a tester installed, for a
  proxy or a capture in front of a scratch bridge. The pinned mTLS inside the stream is unaffected by
  anything in this file: it is not made with the platform trust store and no anchor here can widen it.

  CLEARTEXT: permitted, and this is the line to read carefully.

  What the phone opens at the address in the pairing code is an HTTP Upgrade, and the bridge in this
  repository terminates no TLS of its own - `main.go` hands a bare TCP listener straight to
  `frontdoor`. So the outer hop is `ws://`, and at this targetSdk the platform refuses cleartext by
  default. Before this line, every pairing to a real laptop failed with `CLEARTEXT communication not
  permitted` - and the end-to-end test against a real bridge did not catch it, because it dials
  127.0.0.1 through `adb reverse` and the platform's default configuration carries a loopback
  exemption this file inherits.

  What is given up, stated narrowly rather than waved at:

    - NOT confidentiality. What travels over this socket is TLS 1.3 with a byte-pinned peer, driven
      by TlsDriver INSIDE the upgrade. Nothing readable crosses the network.
    - NOT the trust decision. The outer hop authenticates nobody, by design: the entire reason
      `frontdoor` exists is that a TLS-terminating proxy in the path destroys a client certificate,
      so the mTLS was moved inside where a proxy moves bytes it cannot read.
    - The platform's own belt-and-braces check, which can only see the outer socket and cannot see
      the TLS inside it. That is a real loss, and it is why `CleartextPolicyTest` asserts this rather
      than leaving it to a comment.

  The narrower alternative - naming the laptop's host here - is not available: the address arrives in
  a pairing code at runtime and is deliberately absent from this repository. The alternative worth
  having is a scheme in the pairing payload, so a deployment behind a TLS-terminating proxy says so
  and gets `wss://`. That is a payload version bump on both sides, not a line in this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
